    <Interaction elementId="_2.4.1.46-RXN">
      <Xref identifier="EC-2.4.1.46" dataSource="Enzyme Nomenclature" />
      <Waypoints>
        <Point elementId="_2.4.1.46-RXN_start" x="545.0" y="740" elementRef="DIACYLGLYCEROL" relX="1.0" relY="0.0" />
        <Point elementId="_2.4.1.46-RXN_end" arrowHead="Conversion" x="305.0" y="540" elementRef="D-Galactosyl-12-diacyl-glycerols" relX="-1.0" relY="0.0" />
        <Anchor elementId="_2.4.1.46-RXN_anchor" position="0.5" shapeType="Circle" />
      </Waypoints>
      <Graphics lineColor="000000" lineStyle="Solid" lineWidth="1.0" connectorType="Straight" />
      <Comment source="BioCyc">3-&amp;beta;-D-Galactosyl-1,2-diacylglycerol (monogalactosyldiacylglycerol, MGDG, MGD) is the most abundant chloroplast membrane lipid. There are three known isozymes catalyze the biosynthesis of MGDG in Arabidopsis. MGD1 is the predominate MGDG synthase and it synthesizes both prokaryotic and eukaryotic MGDG with no substrate preference. Whereas MGD2 and MGD3 have higher affinity for eukaryotic diacylglycerol (DAG).</Comment>
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="O81770" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pir" value="T05092" />
